# Production deployment

Use this procedure when the user explicitly asks to deploy or launch the app in production.

The product name is **Tally**. The git repo and local folder stay `loan-tracker` / `finance_tracker`.

- Host: `root@195.201.98.136`
- Domain: `https://tally.ereshchenko.com`
- App directory: `/var/www/tally` (code in `backend/`)
- Service: `tally-api.service` (user `nginx`, `127.0.0.1:5053`, gunicorn `--timeout 120`). Port 5052 is Hinder; do not reuse it.
- Ledger: `/var/www/tally/backend/data/ledger.json` via `DATA_PATH` in `backend/.env`. Git and `deploy-generic.sh` both ignore `data/*.json`. Do not delete or overwrite it on deploy.
- Config: `deploy.conf` in the repo, applied by `~/scripts/deploy-generic.sh`
- `WORKERS=1`. The ledger is a single JSON file that every request reads and rewrites; one worker serialises requests so concurrent requests cannot lose each other's writes.

## Ledger durability (do not regress)

`app.py` treats the ledger as precious. Keep these invariants when touching `load_data`/`save_data`:

- **Atomic writes only.** `save_data` goes through `_write_json_atomic` (temp file in the same dir + `fsync` + `os.replace`). A reader must never be able to observe a truncated/partial file. Never reintroduce `open(DATA_PATH, 'w')` + `json.dump`.
- **Writers are serialised.** `save_data` runs under `ledger_lock()` (process `RLock` + `fcntl.flock` on `<DATA_PATH>.lock`). Held only for the write, never around slow work (model calls, statement parsing).
- **Never wipe on read failure.** `load_data` on a missing file creates the empty default (first run). On an existing but empty/unparseable file it quarantines to `<DATA_PATH>.corrupt-<ts>` and raises `LedgerUnreadableError` — it must NOT overwrite the ledger with defaults.
- **Daily backups.** `_maybe_backup_ledger` snapshots the pre-write state once per UTC day to `<DATA_PATH>.bak-YYYYMMDDTHHMMSSZ`, rotating to `LEDGER_BACKUP_KEEP` (default 7) newest. Restore by copying a `.bak-*` back over `ledger.json`.
- **Backups live outside the app dir in prod.** `backend/.env` sets `LEDGER_BACKUP_DIR=/var/www/tally-backups` (owned `nginx:nginx`, mode `0770`). This matters: `deploy-generic.sh` runs `rsync --delete` over `/var/www/tally/backend`, and its excludes only protect `data/*.json` — a `.bak-*`/`.corrupt-*` file inside `data/` would be deleted on the next deploy. Never point `LEDGER_BACKUP_DIR` back inside the rsynced tree.
- **Symptom to recognise:** ledger reset to a tiny `{"loans":{}, "users":{...}}` default while `/api/spending/months` returns `[]`. Check `journalctl -u tally-api`, `data/*.corrupt-*`, and `data/*.bak-*` before anything else.

### pip 26 install blocker (deploy quirk)

pip 26.x arms an import blocker while installing; setuptools 57.4.0's lazy `distutils.command.build` import trips it and `pip install` aborts with `Blocked import of 'distutils.command.build' after pip install started`. The venv carries a startup hook so the module is preloaded before installation begins:

```
venv/lib/python3.10/site-packages/zzz-preimport-distutils.pth   # contains: import distutils.command.build
```

If the venv is ever deleted/rebuilt, `deploy-generic.sh` will fail at `Setting up Python venv...` until this `.pth` is recreated. The name starts with `zzz` so it runs after `distutils-precedence.pth`. `deploy-generic.sh`'s `chmod -R` leaves the file `750 root:nginx`; that is fine (gunicorn runs as `nginx`).

### Incident: 2026-10-30 → 2026-11-02 total data loss

Two concurrent requests (`POST /api/spending/daily/entry` + `GET /api/spending/daily/status` at 19:41:42 UTC on Oct 30) did an unlocked, non-atomic read‑modify‑write of `ledger.json`. A partial read left the file unparseable; the next read (Nov 2 08:55) hit the old destructive reset and replaced it with an empty default (756 bytes). Nobody read the file in between, so it went unnoticed. Recovered from the pre-cutover copy `/root/loan-tracker/data.json` (Oct 13, 803 KB; ivan + 3 loans + 760 tx). Data created Oct 13–30 is otherwise lost. The hardening above is the permanent fix.

Ongoing deploy (does not rewrite nginx or certificates):

```sh
~/scripts/deploy-generic.sh /home/ivan/finance_tracker --redeploy --yes
```

A full install (`--yes` without `--redeploy`) rewrites nginx and re-runs certbot. Use that only for a first install or when the nginx site itself must change. `--redeploy` still rewrites the systemd unit from `deploy.conf`.

After a deploy, check:

```sh
ssh -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=15 root@195.201.98.136 'systemctl is-active tally-api && curl -fsS -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" https://tally.ereshchenko.com/login'
```

The old public gunicorn on port 8000 is retired. nginx on `:8000` redirects to the HTTPS host. Leave `/root/loan-tracker` on disk as the rollback copy.

Rollback: stop `tally-api`, copy `data/ledger.json` back to `/root/loan-tracker/data.json` if Tally has written since cutover, then start the old gunicorn from `/root/loan-tracker` with `--bind 0.0.0.0:8000 --timeout 120`. Remove the `:8000` redirect first so the port is free.

Do not deploy merely because feature work is complete. Deployment is a production mutation and requires an explicit request from the user. Do not print or commit `.env`.
